ound:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#c9a26b;color:#1b1408;font-weight:400;\">5</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#c9a26b;color:#1b1408;font-weight:400;\">5</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#4c9a4a;color:#fff;font-weight:400;\">1</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#c9a26b;color:#1b1408;font-weight:400;\">5</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"doc-caption\">The same town, as our program sees it</div></div></div><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Number</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it is</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Can you walk on it?</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">0</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">tree</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No, it blocks you</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">grass</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">roof</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No, it blocks you</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">shop</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Opens the shop menu</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">4</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">chest</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Opens the chest</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">5</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">path</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">6</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">cave</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Takes you to Emberfall Cave</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">7</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">water</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No, it blocks you</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Those are the real numbers our program produced. Find the <strong>0</strong>s around the edge: those are the trees that fence you in. The little block of <strong>7</strong>s is the pond. The line of <strong>5</strong>s is the road, and the <strong>3</strong> is the shop door. This grid isn't a drawing of the town any more. It <em>is</em> the town.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How the cutting works</h4><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Slice.</strong> Cut the picture into squares of 16 by 16 pixels.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Spot duplicates.</strong> Compute a fingerprint for each square, just as we did for files. Every grass square is identical, so they all get the same number. Ashford Town is 192 squares, but only 8 different ones.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Label.</strong> A person looks at each different tile once and writes down what it is: \"that's a tree, you can't walk through it\". That is 14 small decisions for this whole game.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Rebuild.</strong> Now the game knows, for any square, what's there and whether you can step on it.</li></ol><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">Why this made old games possible</strong><br>The NES had so little memory that games had to work this way. Storing \"tile 1, tile 1, tile 1\" takes far less space than a full picture. When we rebuild maps from tiles, we're working the way the original designers did.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">From a table cell to a working shop</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Here is one number's whole journey. It's worth following closely, because this is the entire course in miniature:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Where</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1. A fan writes a table</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Iron Sword, damage 8, price 40</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2. Harvester saves the page</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>guide/weapons.html</code> arrives in our folder</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3. Parser reads the table</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>\"price\": 40</code> in <code>parsed.json</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">4. Knowledge graph stores it</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>\"robos:cost\": 40</code>, linked to its source</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">5. Build step copies it into the game</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>{ \"name\": \"Iron Sword\", \"damage\": 8, \"price\": 40 }</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">6. You walk into the shop</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">\"Iron Sword (damage 8): 40 gold\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-in-the-shop.png\" alt=\"Inside the Ember Quest shop, with the Iron Sword listed at 40 gold and the robot's checklist alongside.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">Step 6: the number the fan wrote, on the shop counter.</div></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Nobody typed that price into the game. Change it in the guide, run the steps again, and the shop changes with it. That's why this approach is worth the trouble.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Numbers decide whether the game is fair</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The hero starts with 30 HP. With the Iron Sword, each hit does 8 damage to the Cinder Wyrm and its 40 HP last 5 hits, so the boss gets 4 attacks back at 6 damage each. After a Mossling on the way, the hero wins with 3 HP left. Try it with bare hands, and you lose.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">We never designed that tense finish on purpose. It emerges from the numbers on the fan site. Get one wrong, and the game becomes unwinnable or too easy, which is exactly why the earlier stages are so careful.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What about the art?</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">We own Ember Quest's art because a small program draws it. For a real game we can't reuse the original pictures, so we generate new ones. An AI image model is given a description and a reference, and a second step shrinks the result to the same limited colors and size a game of that era would use. These are called <strong>clean-room</strong> assets, and they are the only images we ever publish.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">A real game needs more</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Ember Quest's rules take under 200 lines of code. A real game has far more: full battle formulas, spells, shops in every town, doors that lead to other maps. For that, you'd use a <strong>game engine</strong> such as Godot, which handles drawing, sound and input so that you only write the rules. Module 7 shows what that looks like.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Build the game from your parsed facts",
            "Look at the numbers behind the shop",
            "Play it yourself"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Build the game from your parsed facts",
              "description": "This cuts your collected maps into tiles, reads your parsed facts, and writes a ready-to-play folder.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/build-game.js ~/ember-quest/collection ~/ember-quest/kgraph/parsed.json ~/ember-quest/game",
              "expected": "`Cut 2 maps into 14 different tiles` and the folder it wrote to."
            },
            {
              "title": "Look at the numbers behind the shop",
              "description": "Open the game's data file and find the weapons. These numbers came from the guide table, not from anyone typing them in. On Windows, open `game-data.json` in a text editor and search for \"weapons\".",
              "code": "grep -A5 '\"Iron Sword\"' ~/ember-quest/game/game-data.json",
              "expected": "The Iron Sword, with `\"damage\": 8` and `\"price\": 40`. That's 40 rather than 45, because the parser trusted the main guide."
            },
            {
              "title": "Play it yourself",
              "description": "Start a second small website for the game, then open http://127.0.0.1:8766/ in your browser. Move with the arrow keys. Walk onto the shop door, use up and down to choose, press Enter to buy and Escape to leave. Press Enter to attack in a battle. Leave this running for Module 6.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/serve.js ~/ember-quest/game 8766",
              "expected": "The game appears in your browser. Try walking into a tree or the pond: the grid says those squares are blocked, so you stop. Try the boss without buying the sword, too.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-fights-the-boss.png",
              "imageAlt": "The hero facing the Cinder Wyrm boss in a battle screen",
              "caption": "The boss battle. Its 40 HP came straight from the monster chart."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why do we turn a map picture into a grid of numbers?",
              "options": [
                "Because a grid of numbers looks more attractive on screen than the picture that it was made from",
                "Because a game cannot tell a path from a pond in a picture, but it can look up each numbered square",
                "Because the law requires that all pictures of a game be converted to numbers before they are used",
                "Because number grids are the only kind of file that a modern web browser is actually able to display"
              ],
              "answer": "Because a game cannot tell a path from a pond in a picture, but it can look up each numbered square",
              "explanation": "Once each square has a number, and each number has a meaning such as \"walkable\" or \"shop door\", the map stops being a picture and becomes something a program can use."
            },
            {
              "question": "Ashford Town has 192 squares but only 8 different tile numbers. Why so few?",
              "options": [
                "Because the program only had time to examine eight of the squares before moving on to the next map",
                "Because the rest of those squares were left blank, and they get filled in later on by the game engine",
                "Because identical squares get the same number, so every patch of grass in the town shares just one",
                "Because a Nintendo Entertainment System cannot ever display more than eight tiles in any single game"
              ],
              "answer": "Because identical squares get the same number, so every patch of grass in the town shares just one",
              "explanation": "Each square gets a fingerprint, and matching fingerprints mean the same tile. A person then only has to label each different tile once."
            },
            {
              "question": "What happens if the Iron Sword's price changes in the guide, and you re-run all of the steps?",
              "options": [
                "Nothing changes in the game, since the prices were typed into its code by hand when it was written",
                "The shop in the game changes to match, because the price flows through each step from the guide",
                "The game refuses to start until a programmer has reviewed the new price and has formally approved it",
                "The old price and the new price are averaged, so that the player sees a compromise between the two"
              ],
              "answer": "The shop in the game changes to match, because the price flows through each step from the guide",
              "explanation": "That is the value of the pipeline. No one hand-copies facts into the game, so the game stays in step with its sources."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-06-checking",
          "title": "Module 6: Stage 5, Checking. A Robot That Plays the Game",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "Watch a robot finish Ember Quest while it checks every promise in a plain-English script. Then learn why a person still needs to watch, with two true stories of bugs that passed every test.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How do you know it works?</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">After you change anything in a game, something else can quietly break. Playing from the start after every change would take forever. So we write down what should happen, and have a robot play through and check.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">The script is written in <strong>Gherkin</strong>, which is close to plain English. It uses three kinds of sentence:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Means</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Given</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Where we start</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Given a new game has started in Ashford Town</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>When</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Something the player does</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">When the hero walks to the weapon shop</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Then</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">What must now be true</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Then the hero has 10 gold</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>And</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One more of the same</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">And the hero carries the \"Iron Sword\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Anyone can read that and say whether it describes the game correctly. No programming needed. Here is the full script for Ember Quest:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Feature: The first adventure in Ember Quest\n\n  Scenario: Buy a sword, open a chest and defeat the boss\n    Given a new game has started in Ashford Town\n    Then the hero has 50 gold\n    When the hero walks to the weapon shop\n    And the hero buys the \"Iron Sword\"\n    Then the hero has 10 gold\n    And the hero carries the \"Iron Sword\"\n    When the hero walks to the town chest\n    Then the hero carries the \"Ember Tonic\"\n    When the hero walks to Emberfall Cave\n    Then the current map is Emberfall Cave\n    When the hero walks to the Mossling\n    And the hero fights until the battle ends\n    Then \"Mossling\" has been defeated\n    When the hero walks to the altar\n    And the hero fights until the battle ends\n    Then \"Cinder Wyrm\" has been defeated\n    And the game shows the victory screen</code></pre></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: the robot plays</h4><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/playthrough.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: a robot walks the hero to the shop, buys the Iron Sword, opens a chest, enters the cave, defeats a Mossling and the Cinder Wyrm boss, with each sentence of its script getting a green tick. The clip then shows the hero standing inside a tree.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The ticks appear live as each sentence is checked. The right-hand panel is the game's real state, read while it runs. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The hero starts in Ashford Town with 50 gold and bare hands. The robot presses the \"Let the robot play\" button.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It walks to the shop door and buys the Iron Sword. Gold falls from 50 to 10, which proves the shop used the guide's price of 40.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It opens the town chest and collects the Ember Tonic.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It walks down the road and into Emberfall Cave, then beats a Mossling, picking up 5 gold.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It reaches the altar and fights the Cinder Wyrm, finishing with only 3 HP left.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">All 17 sentences pass, and the game shows the victory screen.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Finally, a deliberately broken start: the hero stands inside a tree, and yet a check of the map name still passes. More on that below.</li></ol></details></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How the robot \"sees\"</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The robot doesn't look at the screen. It presses keys just as you would, then asks the game a direct question: \"where is the hero, and how much gold do they have?\" You can see those answers changing in the right-hand panel of the clip. This is far more reliable than trying to read pixels.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">Each English sentence is matched to a small piece of code. For example, \"the hero has 10 gold\" looks up the gold and compares it. If it isn't 10, the robot stops and says so:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Expected the gold to be 50 but it is 45</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">That's a real message from a test we run, where we deliberately start the hero with the wrong amount. A check that can't fail is worthless, so we prove that this one can.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Why a person still watches</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Here is the most important lesson in this module. <strong>A test only checks what you thought to ask.</strong></p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/hero-in-a-tree.png\" alt=\"The hero standing on a tree tile in Ashford Town, while the game state reports the correct map name and says the hero is standing on a tree.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The check \"the current map is Ashford Town\" passes. It is true! It just isn't the whole truth.</div></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">This isn't a hypothetical. Both of these happened on the real Final Fantasy build, and every test was green:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What the tests said</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What the video showed</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">The real cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">\"The current map is the first town.\" Pass.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The hero was standing inside a tree at the town gate.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The starting spot was one square off the road. The test only checked the name of the map.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">\"The chest was opened, and the potion is in the bag.\" Pass.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The treasure chests looked like flat teal squares.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A leftover debugging overlay was mixing with the wrong room colors. The game logic was perfect, but the picture wasn't.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">That's why every real test run records a video, and someone looks at it. It happened during this very course, too: every test passed on our first recording, but watching the clip revealed that the robot's checklist was ticking the wrong line whenever a sentence appeared twice. We only found that by looking.</p><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #7ee787;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#7ee787;\">The takeaway</strong><br>Robots are brilliant at checking the same 17 things, the same way, forever. People are brilliant at noticing \"hang on, why is he in a tree?\" You want both.</div></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Let the robot play",
            "Break the game on purpose",
            "Put the hero in a tree"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Let the robot play",
              "description": "With the game still running from Module 5, open http://127.0.0.1:8766/ (reload it if you played already) and click **Let the robot play**. Watch the list on the right.",
              "code": "",
              "expected": "The hero moves by itself, and each line gets a green tick. It finishes with a victory screen. At the end, the hero has 3 HP left.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-wins.png",
              "imageAlt": "The Ember Quest victory screen, with every line of the robot's script ticked in green",
              "caption": "Every sentence checked."
            },
            {
              "title": "Break the game on purpose",
              "description": "See what failure looks like. This changes the starting gold from 50 to 45 in your copy of the data. Reload the page and click the button again. The second command puts it back. On Windows, edit `game-data.json` by hand instead.",
              "code": "sed -i 's/\"startingGold\": 50/\"startingGold\": 45/' ~/ember-quest/game/game-data.json\n\n# when you have finished looking, put it back:\nsed -i 's/\"startingGold\": 45/\"startingGold\": 50/' ~/ember-quest/game/game-data.json",
              "expected": "The second line of the script turns red and says `Expected the gold to be 50 but it is 45`, and the robot stops right there. It's supposed to: the game no longer matches its description."
            },
            {
              "title": "Put the hero in a tree",
              "description": "Open the address below in your browser. It starts the hero on a tree square. Look at the picture, and then think about which checks would catch this and which wouldn't.",
              "code": "http://127.0.0.1:8766/?spawn=10,8",
              "expected": "The hero is standing on a tree. A check of the map name would pass. Can you think of a better check? One answer: \"Then the hero is standing on something walkable\".",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/hero-in-a-tree.png",
              "imageAlt": "The hero standing on a tree tile",
              "caption": "Green test, obvious bug."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "How does the robot find out how much gold the hero has?",
              "options": [
                "It takes a picture of the screen and reads the digits with a text-recognition program every frame",
                "It looks up the price in the original fan website and works out how much the hero ought to have",
                "It presses real keys, then asks the game directly for its current state and compares the answer",
                "It reads a written report that a human tester has filled in after playing that part of the game"
              ],
              "answer": "It presses real keys, then asks the game directly for its current state and compares the answer",
              "explanation": "Asking the game for its state is exact and reliable. Reading pixels from the screen would be fragile, and could break with the smallest visual change."
            },
            {
              "question": "In the Gherkin format, what is the job of a sentence that begins with \"Then\"?",
              "options": [
                "It states something that must now be true, and the test fails if it turns out not to be true",
                "It describes the starting point of the scene, before the player has begun to do anything at all",
                "It records something that the player does, such as walking somewhere or buying an item in a shop",
                "It adds a comment for human readers, which the robot then ignores completely when it runs the script"
              ],
              "answer": "It states something that must now be true, and the test fails if it turns out not to be true",
              "explanation": "Given sets the scene, When is an action, and Then is the promise being checked. Then lines are what actually catch bugs."
            },
            {
              "question": "All of the tests passed, but the hero was standing inside a tree. What is the lesson?",
              "options": [
                "Automated tests are worthless, so they should be replaced entirely with careful human playtesters",
                "The tree must have been drawn in the wrong place, as a passing test proves that the code is correct",
                "The robot must have had a fault, because a correctly working robot would not have passed that check",
                "A test only checks what you asked it to, so people should still watch the recordings for what it missed"
              ],
              "answer": "A test only checks what you asked it to, so people should still watch the recordings for what it missed",
              "explanation": "The check on the map name was true, just incomplete. Robots give you tireless repetition, and people notice what nobody thought to write down. You want both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-07-the-real-thing",
          "title": "Module 7: The Real Thing. What It Took for Final Fantasy",
          "durationMinutes": 15,
          "overview": "The same five stages on a real game: how big it got, where fan guides ran out and what filled the gap, and how to publish your work without publishing anyone else's. Plus a checklist for trying your own favorite game.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Same stages, bigger numbers</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">You now understand everything we did for the 1990 American NES release of <em>Final Fantasy</em>. Here are the real numbers from our own run, beside Ember Quest:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Ember Quest</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Final Fantasy (NES)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Files looked at</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">12</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">82</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Connected facts (nodes)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">25</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1,206</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Monster entries</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">164</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Weapons and armor</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">5</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">80</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Spells</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">68</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Shops</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1, written into the demo</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">50</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Treasure chests</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">251</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Places</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">31</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Story milestones</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">none (skipped the AI step)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">30</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Disagreements between sources</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">5</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">New images generated</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">none (a program draws it)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">50</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Nothing new was needed in kind. There was just a lot more of it.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Where fan guides ran out</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The first coverage report for the real game was sobering. Fans had documented the shops and the story wonderfully, but a game engine needs things that no guide bothers to write down:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">We had from guides</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">But a working game also needs</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Without it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Each monster's HP and rewards</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">How accurately it hits, how hard, how well it dodges</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Battles can't run</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A list of places and pictures of maps</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Exactly which squares can be walked on, every door, every chest's position</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">You can't walk around</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Class descriptions</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Starting stats, and how they grow at each of 50 levels</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Nobody can level up</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Spell names and what they do</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The exact power and accuracy numbers</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Magic can't work</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Nothing at all</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Which monsters can appear in which areas</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No random battles</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">This is the coverage report doing its job. It turned \"we're not done\" into a specific list.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Filling the gap: the game's own data</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Those answers exist in only one place: inside the game. Fans have spent decades documenting the original cartridge's data, and we used those notes to read its tables of numbers into our private folder. That is what a <strong>ROM</strong> and a <strong>disassembly</strong> are for (see Module 2).</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">It also revealed things that nobody would guess, such as actual bugs in the 1990 game. A weapon's chance of a critical hit is meant to come from a number in its entry. Due to a bug, the game reads a different value, based on where that weapon sits on its list. To be true to the original, you have to rebuild the bug, too.</p><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #f0b429;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#f0b429;\">What stays private</strong><br>All of that lives in a private folder on one computer. The game data, the original maps, the guides, the music, and any screenshot or video showing the original art. None of it is in the project's public files, and that's why you don't see a single frame of it in this course.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The real game, and its robot</h4><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Rebuild.</strong> The game was built in the Godot engine, from the facts we gathered.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Remote control.</strong> The game has a small private remote-control connection, so a test can ask for its state and press buttons precisely, in the way our robot does in Ember Quest.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Check.</strong> Gherkin scripts run the game on an invisible screen and record a video of each run. The parser also writes many of these scripts itself: for a new game, it produces checks for starting a game, shops, chests and story milestones from the facts it found.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Look.</strong> Then a person watches. That's how we found the hero in the tree and the teal treasure chests.</li></ul><p class=\"lesson-text\">The robot-tested part of the real game is an opening stretch: forming a party, exploring the first town, meeting the king, looting a temple and defeating its boss. That is the first chapter rather than the whole game, and it's where the bugs in Module 6 turned up.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Publishing only what is yours</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">You've made something, and you'd like to share it. There is exactly one safe way out of your private folder, and it's a command that builds a clean copy:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js publish --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph --to ~/ember-quest/public --kinds none</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">The result holds the parsed facts and any images you generated. It has no source files, no source names and no quotes. It also doesn't upload anything. Putting it online is a separate, deliberate decision you make afterwards.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Try your own favorite game: a checklist</h4><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Pick something small.</strong> An early game with a short story and small maps. Save the epic for later.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Decide on your rules first.</strong> What will you collect, and from where? Do you have permission? What stays private?</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Choose one version.</strong> Be specific about which release you are rebuilding, because different versions have different numbers.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Collect by hand first.</strong> Save the maps and guide pages into one private folder.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Run <code>scan</code>, then <code>parse</code>, then <code>coverage</code>.</strong> Let the report tell you what to gather next.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Read the disagreements.</strong> Settle them yourself. This is where you learn the game.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Rebuild one small slice.</strong> One town and one fight. Make that work, then grow.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Write the checks in plain English,</strong> then have a robot run them, and watch the videos.</li></ol><p class=\"lesson-text\">If you use RobOS, two ready-made skills do much of the work for you: <code>/jrpg-parse-assets</code> reads your folder and reports what's missing, and <code>/jrpg-generate-art</code> makes the clean-room art.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What you can now explain</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">If you've followed this far, you can explain scraping, parsing, knowledge graphs, tile maps and automated playthroughs to a friend, and you've seen each of them really happen. That was the whole goal.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Make a clean copy that is safe to share",
            "Tidy up when you are finished"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Make a clean copy that is safe to share",
              "description": "Build the public copy of your Ember Quest facts. Then search it for the names of your source pages, to confirm they're really gone.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js publish --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph --to ~/ember-quest/public --kinds none\n\ngrep -rl \"weapons.html\" ~/ember-quest/public",
              "expected": "First `Wrote 13 nodes and 0 images`, ending `Nothing was pushed anywhere.` Then the search prints nothing at all, which is the result you want: the public copy never mentions where the facts came from."
            },
            {
              "title": "Tidy up when you are finished",
              "description": "Press Ctrl+C in each terminal window that's still running a small website. If you want the disk space back, delete the `ember-quest` folder in your home folder, using your normal file manager. Everything in it was created by these labs.",
              "code": "",
              "expected": "The windows return to a prompt, and http://127.0.0.1:8765/ stops responding in your browser. That shows it was only ever running on your own computer."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Fan guides gave the real game's monster HP, but not how hard each monster hits. Where did that come from?",
              "options": [
                "From asking an AI model to make a sensible estimate based on how strong each of the monsters looks",
                "From the game's own data tables, read with the help of fans' decades of notes about the cartridge",
                "From a survey of long-time players, who were asked to remember how much damage each monster did",
                "From a later remake of the game, whose battle numbers were simply copied across to the older game"
              ],
              "answer": "From the game's own data tables, read with the help of fans' decades of notes about the cartridge",
              "explanation": "Only the game itself holds those exact numbers. The coverage report pointed to the gap, and the game's internal tables filled it, all kept private."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does the jrpg-parser publish command do with your project?",
              "options": [
                "It uploads your entire working folder to a public website, so that other people are able to play it",
                "It builds a clean copy with only parsed facts and generated images, and it does not upload anything",
                "It sends your project to the owners of the original game so that they can give you their permission",
                "It compresses all of your collected guide pages into one file that is small enough to share by email"
              ],
              "answer": "It builds a clean copy with only parsed facts and generated images, and it does not upload anything",
              "explanation": "It is the one safe way out of the private folder: no source files, no names and no quotes. Putting the result online stays a separate decision."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does the checklist tell you to rebuild one small slice before attempting the whole game?",
              "options": [
                "Because the parser can only ever read one town of a game at a time, so bigger jobs are not possible",
                "Because the rules say a project must be released in several parts, one for each chapter of the story",
                "Because proving that one town and one fight work end to end reveals problems while they're cheap to fix",
                "Because full games take up too much disk space to be stored on the usual kind of home computer today"
              ],
              "answer": "Because proving that one town and one fight work end to end reveals problems while they're cheap to fix",
              "explanation": "A small slice exercises all five stages. Anything wrong in your process shows up quickly, before you've built hundreds of things on top of it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "robos:technology": "RobOS KGraph"
    }
  ],
  "@id": "urn:robos:package:learning",
  "@type": [
    "oslc:ServiceProvider",
    "robos:Package"
  ],
  "dcterms:description": "Interactive developer courses, tutorials, and architectural training modules.",
  "dcterms:title": "Interactive eLearning Curriculums & Knowledge Modules",
  "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
  "robos:nodes": [
    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:getting-started-with-robos",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:description": "Your first hour with RobOS: what it is, how to install only the apps you need, where your data lives and how to back it up, how to change an app by describing it, and how to use AI features safely or not at all. Short lessons, hands-on steps and quizzes that explain every answer.",
      "dcterms:title": "Getting Started with RobOS",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "45 minutes",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "body": "## Three things you do with RobOS\n\n1. **Use** ready-made apps: Point of Sale for a shop or café, Auto Center for a repair shop, Household for family finances, a media library, games, smart-home control and a full developer suite.\n2. **Customize** any app by describing the change you want. You do not need to read its code.\n3. **Build** a new app from a description, the same way the suites were built.\n\n## Why it works: the knowledge graph\n\nEvery RobOS app is described in a **knowledge graph** (KGraph): what the app is, how it works and how to test it. That written-down description is what lets an AI agent change an app safely, and what lets this eLearning app show you courses. You will meet the KGraph again in lesson 4.\n\n## What RobOS is not\n\n| It is | It is not |\n|---|---|\n| Free, open source (Apache-2.0) | A subscription |\n| Your data on your machine | A cloud service that holds your data |\n| AI as an optional extra | An app that stops working without AI |\n\n> The long-term plan: keep and reuse what AI builds, so each new app needs **less** AI, not more.",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "id": "gs-01-what-robos-is",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Use, customize, build: the three ways to work with RobOS.",
            "Every app is described in the knowledge graph, which is what makes changes safe.",
            "Apps run on your computer and work with no AI configured."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open rowbose.com and read the two-move plan under **The vision**.",
            "Pick one app you pay for today and find its RobOS replacement in the App Directory at rowbose.com/apps/."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS is one open-source app suite that replaces the apps you pay for, one at a time, on your own computer. This lesson gives you the map before you install anything.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The app's description in the knowledge graph (KGraph)",
              "explanation": "The KGraph records what the app is, how it works and how to test it. A change starts as an edit to that description.",
              "options": [
                "A cloud copy of your data that the agent edits",
                "The app's description in the knowledge graph (KGraph)",
                "A paid RobOS support plan",
                "A screenshot of the app you send to the agent"
              ],
              "question": "What lets an AI agent change a RobOS app without you reading its code?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "It works normally; AI features are optional extras",
              "explanation": "Every RobOS app must stay fully usable with no agent configured. AI is always behind an explicit action you choose to take.",
              "options": [
                "It opens in a read-only demo mode",
                "Only the developer apps keep working",
                "It works normally; AI features are optional extras",
                "It asks you to subscribe before it starts"
              ],
              "question": "What happens to a RobOS app if you never set up an AI agent?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 1: What RobOS is, and what it is not"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Get the App Manager\n\nDownload RobOS from **rowbose.com/download/**. Pick the file for your computer:\n\n- **Windows**: the setup `.exe`.\n- **Ubuntu GNOME**: the `.deb`. Double-click it, or run `sudo apt install ./RobOS-*-Linux-amd64.deb`.\n- **Other Linux desktops**: the AppImage (needs FUSE 2).\n- **macOS**: the DMG for Intel or Apple Silicon.\n\nThe base download holds the manager and one shared runtime. Apps are separate downloads, so you only pay for what you install.\n\n## Install apps\n\n1. Open **RobOS App Manager**.\n2. Expand a group and tick the apps you want. Ticking a group selects all of its apps.\n3. Check the download size, then choose **Install selected**. Shared pieces download once.\n\nEach app gets its own launcher. On Ubuntu GNOME they appear in a **RobOS Apps** folder in the app grid; on macOS under `~/Applications/RobOS Apps`.\n\n## Remove apps\n\nTick installed apps and choose **Uninstall selected**. **Uninstall RobOS** removes the manager and every app it installed. Your personal settings in `~/.config/robos/` are kept either way.",
          "durationMinutes": 8,
          "id": "gs-02-install",
          "keyPoints": [
            "One manager, then pick only the apps you need.",
            "Uninstalling keeps your settings in ~/.config/robos/."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Download the RobOS App Manager for your operating system from rowbose.com/download/.",
            "Install one app you will really use, for example **RobOS Household** or **RobOS eLearning**.",
            "Launch it from its own launcher, not from the App Manager."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS installs as one small manager, the RobOS App Manager, and you download only the apps you want from it.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "They stay in ~/.config/robos/",
              "explanation": "Removing apps or the whole manager keeps ~/.config/robos/, so reinstalling picks up where you left off.",
              "options": [
                "They stay in ~/.config/robos/",
                "They are deleted with the apps",
                "They are uploaded to rowbose.com first",
                "They move to the Trash"
              ],
              "question": "You uninstall RobOS from the App Manager. What happens to your personal app settings?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Apps are separate downloads you choose in the App Manager",
              "explanation": "The manager ships with one shared runtime. Each app downloads only when you select it, and shared pieces download once.",
              "options": [
                "Only a trial version is included",
                "Apps are separate downloads you choose in the App Manager",
                "The apps are compressed until you pay",
                "Apps stream from the cloud while you use them"
              ],
              "question": "Why is the base download small?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 2: Install RobOS and your first apps"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Local first\n\nApp settings and data live under `~/.config/robos/` on your machine. Nothing is sent to a RobOS server, because there isn't one.\n\nPasswords and API keys are kept in **pass**, the standard Unix password store, encrypted with your GPG key. You can add a YubiKey or another FIDO2 key for extra protection.\n\n## Back up with Config Sync\n\nOpen **RobOS Preferences → Config Sync & Sharing** and point it at a folder in a cloud drive you already use. You can create two kinds of target:\n\n- A **vault**: a full backup of your settings. It syncs each file both ways, shows you conflicts and keeps restore points.\n- A **share**: a read-only bundle of selected settings, for example a product catalog, that someone else can merge into their own lists.\n\nCredentials are stripped by name and are **never** uploaded, in either case.\n\n```text\nyour cloud folder/\n  robos-config.json   ← what is in the backup\n  blobs/              ← the file contents\n```",
          "durationMinutes": 7,
          "id": "gs-03-your-data",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Your data is files under ~/.config/robos/ on your computer.",
            "A vault is a full backup; a share is a read-only bundle for someone else.",
            "Passwords and keys never leave pass, and are never uploaded."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open `~/.config/robos/` in your file manager and find the folder of an app you installed.",
            "Open **RobOS Preferences → Config Sync & Sharing** and connect a vault to a folder in your cloud drive.",
            "Change one setting in an app, then check that the vault shows the change."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS keeps your data in files on your own computer. Config Sync lets you back it up, or share part of it, through a cloud folder you already own.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "A share",
              "explanation": "A share is a read-only bundle of selected settings that the other person merges into their own lists. A vault is your full private backup.",
              "options": [
                "A restore point",
                "A pass entry",
                "A vault",
                "A share"
              ],
              "question": "You want a friend to get your café menu but not the rest of your settings. Which Config Sync target do you use?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Strips them and never uploads them",
              "explanation": "Credentials are removed by key name before anything is written to the cloud folder.",
              "options": [
                "Strips them and never uploads them",
                "Uploads them only to shares",
                "Encrypts them and uploads them to the vault",
                "Asks you each time"
              ],
              "question": "What does Config Sync do with credentials such as API keys?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 3: Where your data lives, and how to back it up"
        },
        {
          "body": "## The flow\n\n1. **Describe the change.** For example: *Add an allowance envelope for each kid that tops up every Friday and shows on the accounts page.*\n2. **The knowledge graph changes first.** RobOS plans the change as new and changed nodes, such as `Envelope`, `TopUpSchedule` and a new test, before any code is written.\n3. **An agent builds it on a branch.** Your current app is untouched while it works.\n4. **Tests and a walkthrough prove it.** The agent runs the app headlessly and hands back a narrated walkthrough of the new feature.\n5. **You decide.** Keep the change, or throw it away.\n\n## Which agent?\n\nRobOS uses the coding agent you already have: Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI. You sign in to it once and choose it as the active agent. RobOS runs it for you.\n\n## Why the graph comes first\n\nBecause the change is written down before it is built, you can review *what* will change in plain terms, and the tests that come with it check the result. That is the difference between describing a change and hoping an AI got it right.",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "id": "gs-04-customize",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Describe → graph edit → agent builds on a branch → tests and walkthrough → you keep or discard.",
            "Nothing changes in your working app until you keep the change.",
            "You bring your own coding agent."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Write down one small change you would like in an app you use (one sentence is enough).",
            "Read the **Build apps** guide at rowbose.com/build-apps/ and find the step where the knowledge graph is edited.",
            "If you have a coding agent installed, open **Agents Manager** and check that it shows up."
          ],
          "overview": "In RobOS you change an app by saying what you want. The request becomes an edit to the app's knowledge graph, an agent builds it, and tests prove it before you keep it.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The app's knowledge graph",
              "explanation": "The request becomes an edit to the KGraph first, so the change is planned and reviewable before an agent writes code.",
              "options": [
                "The app's code on your main branch",
                "The App Manager",
                "The app's knowledge graph",
                "Your saved data"
              ],
              "question": "What is the first thing that changes when you ask for a new feature?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Throw the change away; your app is unchanged",
              "explanation": "The agent works on a branch. You only keep the change if the tests and walkthrough convince you.",
              "options": [
                "Restore your computer from a backup",
                "Reinstall the app",
                "Nothing; the change is already live",
                "Throw the change away; your app is unchanged"
              ],
              "question": "An agent finished your change and the walkthrough looks wrong. What do you do?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "The ones you already use, such as Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI",
              "explanation": "RobOS has no model of its own to sell you. It runs the agent CLI you sign in to.",
              "options": [
                "The ones you already use, such as Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI",
                "Only a RobOS-hosted model",
                "None; you must write the code yourself",
                "Only agents bought through RobOS"
              ],
              "question": "Which coding agents can RobOS run for you?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 4: Change an app by describing it"
        },
        {
          "body": "## How AI shows up in an app\n\nAn AI feature sits behind an explicit action: a text box with a **✦** button that starts disabled until you type. Nothing is sent until you press it. You pick which agent answers with the agent selector next to it.\n\nExamples:\n\n- **Audio Player**: describe a mood and an AI Playlist picks and orders songs, or turn on the Virtual DJ, which writes short intros between songs.\n- **Household**: ask questions about your own statements.\n\nLeave these alone and the apps work as ordinary apps.\n\n## The Prompt Security Guard\n\nBefore any prompt reaches an agent, RobOS checks it for secrets (cloud keys, tokens, private keys), personal data (card numbers, SSNs) and prompt-injection attempts. You choose what it does when it finds something:\n\n| Mode | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `redact` | The secret is replaced before sending |\n| `block` | The prompt is not sent |\n| `warn` | You are warned and can decide |\n| `audit-only` | It is sent and logged |\n| `off` | No checks |\n\nFindings are logged in `~/.config/robos/prompt-security-audit.json`.\n\n## Local models\n\nIf you would rather not use a cloud agent at all, RobOS can answer one-shot requests with a local model through Ollama. That API mode is **off by default**; you turn it on in RobOS Preferences.",
          "durationMinutes": 8,
          "id": "gs-05-ai-safely",
          "keyPoints": [
            "AI features start only when you press ✦.",
            "You choose the agent for each feature.",
            "The Prompt Security Guard checks every prompt before it is sent."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open RobOS Preferences and find where the Prompt Security Guard mode is set.",
            "Pick the mode you are comfortable with (`redact` is a sensible start).",
            "Optional: try an AI feature such as an AI Playlist, then look for its entry in the audit log."
          ],
          "overview": "Some RobOS apps have optional AI features, like the Audio Player's Virtual DJ. You always start them yourself, you choose the agent, and a prompt guard checks what is sent.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The key is replaced before the prompt is sent",
              "explanation": "Redact mode removes the secret and sends the rest. Block mode would stop the prompt entirely.",
              "options": [
                "The app closes",
                "The key is replaced before the prompt is sent",
                "The prompt is sent as typed and logged",
                "The key is sent but encrypted"
              ],
              "question": "Your prompt accidentally contains an AWS secret key and the guard is in redact mode. What happens?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Don't press its ✦ buttons; there is nothing else to switch off",
              "explanation": "AI only runs when you start it. An app with no agent configured is a normal app.",
              "options": [
                "Disconnect from the internet",
                "Uninstall the app's AI plug-in",
                "Don't press its ✦ buttons; there is nothing else to switch off",
                "Cancel a RobOS AI subscription"
              ],
              "question": "How do you stop an app from ever calling AI?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 5: Use AI features safely, or not at all"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Where courses come from\n\nCourses are `robos:ELearning` nodes in the **learning** package of the knowledge graph (`.robos/kgraphs/learning/package.jsonld`). Each lesson has a summary, lesson text, key ideas, lab steps and quiz questions with explanations.\n\n## Edit a course\n\nOpen a course and choose **Edit course**. Use the **⋯** menu on any lesson to edit it, add a lesson after it or delete it. Then **Save course** writes it back to the knowledge graph and to `.robos/elearning.yaml`.\n\n## Share a course\n\n- **Export website** turns the course into a static HTML page you can host anywhere, for example GitHub Pages.\n- **Export as HTML Zip** on a lesson packages that one lesson for offline use.\n- The same exporter runs from a terminal:\n\n```bash\nnode packages/robos-elearning/cli-export-web.js --course=getting-started-with-robos --output=course.html\n```\n\n## Have an agent write one\n\nThe `generate-app-elearning` agent skill reads an app from the knowledge graph and drafts a course for it: lessons, labs and quizzes. Review it here, edit what is wrong and save.\n\n## Your progress and certificate\n\nProgress is saved on this computer in `~/.config/robos/elearning/progress.json`, so you can stop at any time. Finish every lesson, lab and quiz to get a certificate you can save as a PDF.",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "id": "gs-06-keep-learning",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Courses are knowledge-graph nodes you can edit and save.",
            "Export a course as a website, or one lesson as an offline zip.",
            "Progress is saved locally; finishing earns a PDF certificate."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Turn on **Edit course** in this course and open the **⋯** menu on this lesson.",
            "Choose **Copy as Markdown** and paste it somewhere to see the lesson's source.",
            "Finish this course and save your certificate as a PDF."
          ],
