    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "How to Rebuild a Classic Video Game: A Beginner's Guide to Scraping, Knowledge Graphs and Robot Playtesters",
      "dcterms:description": "A from-zero, plain-English course. Follow a tiny made-up game through every stage, with real screen recordings: collect a fan site, pull out the facts, connect them into a knowledge graph, rebuild a playable game, and have a robot play it. Then see what the same steps produced for the real Final Fantasy on the NES.",
      "robos:topic": "Scraping, Parsing, Knowledge Graphs & Automated Playthroughs, Explained from Zero",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:level": "Beginner",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Curious beginners and game fans. No programming needed to follow along.",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "130 minutes",
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:targetApplication": "urn:robos:library:robos-jrpg-parser",
      "robos:documentationPage": "docs/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough.md",
      "robos:webUrl": "/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough/",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:modulesCount": 7,
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-04T21:00:00.000Z",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "mod-01-start-here",
          "title": "Module 1: Start Here. What Are We Actually Doing?",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "The whole project in plain English, before any technical words: what we are building, the five stages, and the tiny made-up game that will be our example all the way through.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The idea in one paragraph</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Imagine a much-loved board game that went out of print decades ago. You can't buy it, but fans have written about it for years: photos of the board, lists of every card, advice on how to win. If you gathered all of that and read it carefully, you could rebuild the game.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">This course does the same thing with an old video game. A computer helps us <strong>collect</strong> what fans wrote, <strong>read</strong> it, <strong>organize</strong> it into a tidy set of facts, <strong>rebuild</strong> the game from those facts, and then <strong>check</strong> the result by having a robot play it from start to finish.</p><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">You do not need to be a programmer</strong><br>You can follow the whole course by reading it and watching the clips. The hands-on labs are optional, and each one tells you exactly what to type and what you should see.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The five stages</h4><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin:14px 0;align-items:stretch;\"><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 1</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Collect</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Gather maps and guide pages into one folder. Copying them with a program is called <em>scraping</em>.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 2</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Read</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">A program called a <em>parser</em> pulls facts out of those messy pages.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 3</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Organize</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">The facts go into a <em>knowledge graph</em>: every fact connected to the things it relates to.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 4</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Rebuild</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Turn the facts into maps, shops and battles you can actually play.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 5</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Check</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">A robot plays the game like a person would and reports anything that's wrong.</div></div></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">That is the entire course. Every module that follows is one of these stages, explained slowly, with something real to look at.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Our example: Ember Quest</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A real game is too big to hold in your head while you are learning, and examples quickly turn abstract. So we made up a game small enough to see all of it at once. <strong>Ember Quest does not exist.</strong> We invented its town, its cave, its one boss and its fan website for this course.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Places you can walk around</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2 (Ashford Town and Emberfall Cave)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Weapons for sale</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Monsters</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2, plus 1 boss</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Fan website pages to collect</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">7 guide pages and 3 maps</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Everything you see in this course's clips really ran: the real download tool, the real parser, and a real game built from their output. Using an invented game means we own every pixel, so we can show you all of it.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-in-the-shop.png\" alt=\"Ember Quest running in a browser. The hero stands at the shop door, and the robot's script is ticking off steps beside the game.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">Where we end up: Ember Quest, built from a fan site's tables, being played by a robot.</div></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">And then the real thing</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Once you understand the small version, Module 7 shows what the same stages produced for a real game, the 1990 American release of <em>Final Fantasy</em> for the Nintendo Entertainment System: 1,206 connected facts, and a playable version a robot could fight its way through.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How to use this course</h4><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Short on time?</strong> Read Module 1, watch the clips in Modules 3, 4 and 6, then read Module 7.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>New to all this?</strong> Module 2 explains every word you'll meet. Keep it open in another tab.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Want to try it?</strong> Do the labs. They run on your own computer and never touch anyone else's website.</li></ul></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Name the five stages from memory",
            "Look at the finished example and spot where each stage shows up"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Name the five stages from memory",
              "description": "No computer needed. Close this page, or just look away, and try to say the five stages in order, in your own words. A good version sounds like explaining it to a friend over coffee.",
              "code": "",
              "expected": "Collect, read, organize, rebuild, check. If you said \"copy the fan pages, pull out the facts, connect them up, make the game, let a robot test it\", that is exactly right."
            },
            {
              "title": "Look at the finished example and spot where each stage shows up",
              "description": "Look at the picture below. The town map came from the Collect stage. The price on the sword came from Read and Organize. The game itself is Rebuild. The green ticks are Check.",
              "code": "",
              "expected": "You can point at something in the picture for each stage. The next modules explain how each one got there.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-in-the-shop.png",
              "imageAlt": "Ember Quest with its shop menu open, next to the robot's checklist"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Which description best matches what this course teaches you to do?",
              "options": [
                "Rebuild an old game by collecting what fans wrote, organizing it into facts, and checking it with a robot",
                "Copy an old game's disc onto a modern computer so that it runs exactly as it did in 1990",
                "Ask an AI model to invent a brand new game that merely looks similar to an old classic one",
                "Download a finished remake that someone else already made and change the colors on its menus"
              ],
              "answer": "Rebuild an old game by collecting what fans wrote, organizing it into facts, and checking it with a robot",
              "explanation": "The project works from descriptions of the game. It gathers them, turns them into organized facts, builds from those facts, and verifies the result by playing it."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does this course use a made-up game called Ember Quest for its examples?",
              "options": [
                "Because the real tools are not able to read anything about a commercially released video game",
                "Because it is small enough to see completely, and we own all of it so we can show every part",
                "Because a real game would have to be bought first, and the course wants to stay completely free",
                "Because Ember Quest is a famous 1989 classic that many readers will already remember playing"
              ],
              "answer": "Because it is small enough to see completely, and we own all of it so we can show every part",
              "explanation": "A tiny invented game keeps examples concrete and lets the course publish every map and page. Real game material stays private because we do not have the rights to it."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does the robot do in the final stage of the project?",
              "options": [
                "It scans the internet looking for any fan websites that have not been collected into the folder",
                "It writes new story chapters for the game whenever the original guide pages run out of material",
                "It plays the rebuilt game by pressing keys and checks that what happens matches what should happen",
                "It paints new artwork for each town using the colors that it finds in the collected map pictures"
              ],
              "answer": "It plays the rebuilt game by pressing keys and checks that what happens matches what should happen",
              "explanation": "The robot is a tester. It presses the same keys a person would and compares the game's state against a written list of expectations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-02-before-you-begin",
          "title": "Module 2: Before You Begin. The Words and Ideas You Will Need",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "A from-zero primer. What makes up a role-playing game, how computers store the things we will collect, what the long words in this course's title mean, the rules we follow, and how to set up your computer if you want to do the labs.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 1: What is inside an old role-playing game?</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>JRPG</strong> (Japanese role-playing game) is a story-driven game where you walk a small party of heroes around a world, buy equipment, and fight monsters in turns. It sounds rich, but underneath, it's surprisingly simple. Almost everything is a list of numbers plus a few rules.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">HP (hit points)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">How much damage something can take before it is defeated.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Mossling has 12 HP. The Cinder Wyrm has 40.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Stats</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The numbers that describe a character, monster or item.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Iron Sword does 8 damage and costs 40 gold.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Class</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A type of hero, which decides what they can use.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Only the Warden can use an Iron Sword.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Tile</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One small square of a map, like a single floor tile in a bathroom.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Every map is 16 tiles wide and 12 tall. Each tile is 16 pixels square.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Sprite</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A small picture of a character or thing that moves around.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The little hero in the red cap.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Boss</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A tough, one-off enemy that usually ends a chapter.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Cinder Wyrm, waiting on the altar.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Walkthrough</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A fan-written guide that explains how to finish the game.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The site's Walkthrough page.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">Why old games work well for this</strong><br>The Nintendo Entertainment System (NES), sold from the mid-1980s, had very little memory. Its games had to be built from tiny repeating tiles and short tables of numbers. That simplicity is what makes it possible for fans to document a whole game, and for us to rebuild one.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 2: How computers store what we collect</h4><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">An everyday comparison</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">File and folder</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A file is one saved thing. A folder holds files.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A document and a filing cabinet drawer.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Web page (HTML)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A file of text with labels saying \"this is a heading\" or \"this is a table\". Your browser turns it into what you see.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A typed page with instructions for how to lay it out.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Link</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A part of a web page that points to another address.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A page reference: \"see page 40\".</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">PNG</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A common format for picture files.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A photo print, but made of exact tiny squares.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Program or script</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A list of instructions a computer follows.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A recipe.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Terminal</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A window where you type instructions as text instead of clicking.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Texting your computer.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Command</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One instruction you type in the terminal.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One text message.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">localhost (127.0.0.1)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">An address meaning \"this computer\". A website here is only visible to you.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Talking to yourself in your own kitchen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">One more format matters a lot, because the rest of the course leans on it. <strong>JSON</strong> is a way of writing down facts that both people and programs can read. Here are the main lines of the entry our parser produced for the Iron Sword:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>{\n  \"name\": \"Iron Sword\",\n  \"kind\": \"weapon\",\n  \"damage\": 8,\n  \"price\": 40,\n  \"source\": \"guide/weapons.html\"\n}</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Each line is a label and a value. That's all it is. Notice the last line: every fact remembers <strong>where it came from</strong>. This idea will save us more than once.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 3: The words in this course's title</h4><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Scraping</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\"><strong>Scraping</strong> means having a program visit web pages and save a copy of what is there, instead of a person clicking \"Save\" over and over. The program that does this here is called the <strong>harvester</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Parsing</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\"><strong>Parsing</strong> means reading messy material and pulling out structured facts. You do it yourself when you read a restaurant menu and note down \"soup, $6\". Our <strong>parser</strong> does it to guide pages.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Knowledge graph (kgraph)</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>knowledge graph</strong> is a set of facts stored as a web of connections rather than a pile of separate lists. Think of a family tree, or a detective's corkboard with string running between photos. Each item is a <strong>node</strong>, and each string between them is a <strong>link</strong>.</p><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A small knowledge graph with Iron Sword in the middle, linked to its price, damage, the class that can use it and the guide page it came from\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:12px auto;font-family:Verdana,sans-serif;\">\n<defs><marker id=\"arr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#8b949e\"/></marker></defs>\n<g stroke=\"#8b949e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"62\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"530\" y2=\"62\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"218\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"530\" y2=\"218\"/></g>\n<g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c9d1d9\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"225\" y=\"78\">costs</text><text x=\"455\" y=\"78\">does damage</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"205\">can be used by</text><text x=\"455\" y=\"205\">the fact came from</text></g>\n<g font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">\n<rect x=\"262\" y=\"118\" width=\"156\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#00bcd4\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#04202a\">Iron Sword</text>\n<rect x=\"60\" y=\"38\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1c2128\" stroke=\"#30363d\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"62\" fill=\"#f0f6fc\">40 gold</text>\n<rect x=\"510\" y=\"38\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1c2128\" stroke=\"#30363d\"/><text x=\"565\" y=\"62\" fill=\"#f0f6fc\">8 points</text>\n<rect x=\"60\" y=\"204\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1c2128\" stroke=\"#30363d\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"228\" fill=\"#f0f6fc\">Warden class</text>\n<rect x=\"490\" y=\"204\" width=\"150\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1c2128\" stroke=\"#30363d\"/><text x=\"565\" y=\"228\" fill=\"#f0f6fc\">The weapons page</text>\n</g></svg><p class=\"lesson-text\">Once facts are connected, a program can answer questions a flat list can't, such as \"which weapons can a Warden afford in the first town, and can we prove where each price came from?\"</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Automated playthrough</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>playthrough</strong> is playing a game from start to finish. <strong>Automated</strong> means a robot does it. The robot follows a script written in near-normal English using a format called <strong>Gherkin</strong>, run by a tool called <strong>Cucumber</strong>. Scripts read like this:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>When the hero walks to the weapon shop\nAnd the hero buys the \"Iron Sword\"\nThen the hero has 10 gold</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">If the hero doesn't end up with exactly 10 gold, the test fails and tells you so.</p></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 4: A few words you'll meet later</h4><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Game engine (Godot)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Ready-made software for building games, handling drawing, sound and input. Godot is a free one. We used it for the real Final Fantasy build.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">ROM</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A copy of the data on an old game cartridge.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Disassembly</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A game's internal instructions, turned back into a form people can read and annotate.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Clean-room art</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">New artwork we generate ourselves instead of reusing the original game's pictures.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">AI agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A language model given a specific job. Here it reads long passages of guide text and reports what it finds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 5: The ground rules</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Fans wrote those guides, and companies own those games. Being able to copy something doesn't make it yours. We hold ourselves to these rules. They are our project's practice rather than legal advice, so check what applies to you.</p><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>People collect from real fan sites by hand.</strong> We do not point automated downloaders at real fan sites. The harvester enforces this itself: it refuses any address outside your own computer, so in this course it only ever talks to the practice site.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>What you collect stays private.</strong> Guide pages, map pictures and game data live in a private folder. They are never uploaded or published.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>We only publish what we made ourselves:</strong> the organized facts and newly generated art, with source names and quotes removed. A special <code>publish</code> command makes that clean copy (see Module 7).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>If you ever write your own scraper for a site that gave you permission,</strong> read its rules first, say honestly who you are, go slowly, and stop if you are asked to.</li></ul><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #f0b429;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#f0b429;\">Why you don't see any real fan site in this course</strong><br>A screen recording of someone's website is a copy of that website. Publishing one here would break our second rule. That's why every clip shows our own practice site, which behaves the same way and belongs to us.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 6: Set up your computer (only if you want the labs)</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">You need three free tools: <strong>Git</strong> (downloads the project), <strong>Node.js</strong> and <strong>Python 3</strong> (run the programs). On Linux and Mac you may already have some of them. The lab below checks.</p><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">Keep two terminal windows handy</strong><br>In Module 3, one window will run the practice website and must stay open. You will type everything else in the second window. If a command says \"not found\", you are probably in the wrong folder: type <code>cd robos</code> first.</div></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Check that the tools are installed",
            "Download the project",
            "Run the course's checks to prove everything works"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Check that the tools are installed",
              "description": "Open a terminal and paste this in, then press Enter. Each command asks a tool to report its version. No harm can come from it.",
              "code": "git --version\nnode --version\npython3 --version",
              "expected": "Three lines with version numbers, such as `v22.22.1` for Node. Node 18 or newer and Python 3.8 or newer are what you want. If a line says \"command not found\", install that tool from its official website and try again."
            },
            {
              "title": "Download the project",
              "description": "This copies the RobOS project into a new folder called `robos`, then moves your terminal into that folder. It downloads a lot of files and can take a few minutes.",
              "code": "git clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git\ncd robos",
              "expected": "Some progress lines, ending with your terminal back at a prompt. All later labs assume you are inside this `robos` folder."
            },
            {
              "title": "Run the course's checks to prove everything works",
              "description": "The project includes a test that runs this whole course's example from start to finish in a temporary folder. It uses only your own computer.",
              "code": "node --test packages/robos-test/tests/jrpg-parser/practice-site.test.js",
              "expected": "After a few seconds you should see `# pass 6` and `# fail 0`. If so, your computer can do every lab in this course."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "What does it mean to say that a program is \"parsing\" a guide page?",
              "options": [
                "It is reading the messy page and pulling out structured facts such as names, prices and damage",
                "It is making a perfect backup copy of the page so that the page cannot ever be lost or changed",
                "It is uploading the page to a public website so that other players are able to read it as well",
                "It is checking whether the person who wrote the page has given permission for it to be copied"
              ],
              "answer": "It is reading the messy page and pulling out structured facts such as names, prices and damage",
              "explanation": "Parsing turns material written for people into structured facts for programs, just as you might read a menu and write down \"soup, $6\"."
            },
            {
              "question": "What makes a knowledge graph different from a simple list of items?",
              "options": [
                "A knowledge graph can only be stored online, whereas a simple list is kept on your own computer",
                "A knowledge graph connects each fact to related things, so you can follow the links and ask questions",
                "A knowledge graph always holds much more text than a list, because every item includes a full essay",
                "A knowledge graph sorts its items alphabetically, whereas a simple list keeps them in random order"
              ],
              "answer": "A knowledge graph connects each fact to related things, so you can follow the links and ask questions",
              "explanation": "The links are the point. Because the Iron Sword connects to its price, its class and its source page, a program can follow those strings to answer connected questions."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does every clip in this course show a practice website rather than a real fan site?",
              "options": [
                "Real fan sites have been shut down, so there are no more of them left that anyone could record",
                "The harvester program is unable to read pages unless they are stored on the very same computer",
                "A recording of a site copies it, and we only publish material that we made and have the right to share",
                "A practice site loads quite a lot faster, which makes the recorded video clips look much smoother"
              ],
              "answer": "A recording of a site copies it, and we only publish material that we made and have the right to share",
              "explanation": "A screen recording of someone else's site would republish their work. Using our own invented site means every pixel is ours to show."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-03-collecting",
          "title": "Module 3: Stage 1, Collecting. What Scraping Actually Looks Like",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "Watch a real scrape happen on screen. A person browses a fan site, the harvester works out what to download, and the files arrive in a folder one by one, while the website's own log shows what it saw.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What we need, and why one page is not enough</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Think about what it would take to rebuild a game. A walkthrough tells you the story and where to go. It won't tell you where every wall stands or how hard a monster hits. So we gather different kinds of material:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">We collect</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Which gives us</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Map pictures</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">What each place looks like, tile by tile</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3 PNG files</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Weapon and armor tables</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Prices and strengths</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Weapons page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Monster charts</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">HP, attack, rewards</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Monsters page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Treasure lists</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">What is in each chest, and where</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Treasure page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Walkthrough</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The order of events</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Walkthrough page</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">Meet the practice site</strong><br>The clips below use \"The Ember Quest Archive\", a made-up fan site running on this one computer. Its address, <code>127.0.0.1:8765</code>, means \"this machine\". Nothing in these clips touches the internet.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: finding what to download</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Real sites are messy. Our atlas page lists maps for two games, and we only want one. Before downloading anything, the harvester reads the page and writes a <strong>manifest</strong>, which is just a shopping list of files.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scrape-discover.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: a browser opens the Ember Quest Archive, clicks through to the map atlas, marks three Ember Quest maps to keep and two Star Knight maps to ignore, then the harvester writes a list of the three files.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">A real recording of an embedded browser and the real harvester program, running together. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">A person opens the fan site in a browser and has a look around. The panel at the bottom right is the website's own log: every line is something it was asked for.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">They click through to the map atlas, which lists maps for two different games.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The rule is simple: keep files whose names start with <code>EmberQuest-</code>. Green rows match it, red rows don't.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The harvester runs. It fetches that one page, finds the links, and applies the same rule. It downloads nothing yet.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The shopping list holds exactly three maps, with nothing from the other game.</li></ol></details></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The command, explained word by word</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The command in that clip looks intimidating, but each part is just a piece of a sentence:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>python3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --vgmaps-index http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --map-prefix EmberQuest- \\\n  --discover-only</code></pre><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">This part</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>python3 ...harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Run the harvester program.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--out-dir</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Put everything in this folder.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--vgmaps-index</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">This is the page that lists the maps.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--base-url</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Links on that page are relative to this address.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--map-prefix EmberQuest-</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Only keep files whose names start like this.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--discover-only</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Just write the list. Do not download yet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">The harvester also has a built-in limit: it refuses any address outside your own computer, including links and redirects that try to lead somewhere else. Real websites are collected by a person, by hand.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: downloading everything</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The harvester can find maps by itself, but it has no way to know which guide pages matter. So a person lists those. Then the harvester works through the whole list.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scrape-download.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: the harvester downloads ten files one at a time, with a line printed for each, while the collection folder listing fills up from empty to ten files.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The folder at the bottom right starts empty and fills up as each file arrives. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">A person adds the guide pages to the list. The collection folder is empty at this point.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The harvester fetches each file in turn and pauses between requests, so it isn't hammering the site.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Each line reports the file, its size, its picture dimensions if it has any, and its fingerprint (<code>sha256</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The folder listing grows with every download.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It finishes with 10 files and writes a receipt.</li></ol></details><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">What is that sha256 thing?</strong><br>It is a <strong>fingerprint</strong> of a file: a short code computed from its contents. Change a single letter and the code changes completely. The receipt records one for each file, so later on we can prove exactly which version of each page our facts came from.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What we have at the end</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A private folder, with no game in it yet:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>ember-quest/collection/\n  EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png      &lt;- town map picture\n  EmberQuest-EmberfallCave.png    &lt;- cave map picture\n  EmberQuest-World.png            &lt;- world map picture\n  guide/weapons.html              &lt;- the main price list\n  guide/shopkeeper-notes.html     &lt;- a player's notes (is it right?)\n  guide/anniversary-prices.html   &lt;- about a remake (a trap!)\n  guide/bestiary.html             &lt;- monster chart\n  guide/chests.html               &lt;- treasure list\n  guide/magic.html                &lt;- spell table\n  guide/walkthrough.html          &lt;- the story\n  sources-manifest.json           &lt;- the shopping list\n  download-receipt.json           &lt;- proof of what we got</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Notice that two of those pages are suspicious. One is a player's memory of the prices. One describes a different version of the game. A careless program would take them at face value. Module 4 shows how the parser catches both.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Create the practice website",
            "Start it, and look at it in your browser",
            "Run the harvester to write the shopping list",
            "Add the guide pages and download everything"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Create the practice website",
              "description": "This writes the made-up fan site into a new folder called `ember-quest` in your home folder. Nothing goes online.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/practice-site.js ~/ember-quest/site",
              "expected": "One line: `Wrote the practice site to` followed by the folder."
            },
            {
              "title": "Start it, and look at it in your browser",
              "description": "This starts the website on your own computer. It keeps running, so leave this window alone. It will print a line for every request, which is the same log you saw in the clip. Now open http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ in your normal web browser.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/serve.js ~/ember-quest/site 8765",
              "expected": "The terminal says `Serving ... at http://127.0.0.1:8765`, and your browser shows the map atlas with five maps from two games. **Open a second terminal window for the next steps**, and type `cd robos` in it.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/atlas-keep-and-ignore.png",
              "imageAlt": "The map atlas page with the three Ember Quest maps marked keep and two Star Knight maps marked ignore",
              "caption": "The site as seen in the clip. The green and red marks were added for the recording, so your copy will not have them."
            },
            {
              "title": "Run the harvester to write the shopping list",
              "description": "In the second window, run this. It reads the atlas page and writes the list. Look at the first window afterwards: it shows that the harvester asked for just one page.",
              "code": "python3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --vgmaps-index http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --map-prefix EmberQuest- \\\n  --discover-only",
              "expected": "A line starting `[DISCOVER] Wrote`, ending `(3 map PNGs, 0 wiki pages)`. It found three maps out of the five listed. The \"0 wiki pages\" is fine, because you will add those in the next step.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/harvester-shopping-list.png",
              "imageAlt": "The harvester reporting three maps found, followed by a list of the three Ember Quest files",
              "caption": "The same step in the recording."
            },
            {
              "title": "Add the guide pages and download everything",
              "description": "The first command adds the seven guide pages to the list. The second downloads the whole list, pausing half a second between files.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/collect.js ~/ember-quest/collection\n\npython3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --manifest ~/ember-quest/collection/sources-manifest.json",
              "expected": "First `The manifest now lists 3 maps and 7 guide pages.`, then ten lines starting `[DOWNLOADED]`, then `[DONE] Verified 10 files`. If you run it again, those lines say `[CACHED]` instead, because the harvester doesn't download what it already has.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/download-finished.png",
              "imageAlt": "The harvester having downloaded ten files, with the collection folder listing all of them",
              "caption": "Ten files, each with its fingerprint."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "What is the manifest that the harvester writes before downloading anything?",
              "options": [
                "It is a shopping list of the exact files to fetch, so that we can check the list before downloading",
                "It is a legal document that gives the harvester permission to make copies of files from a website",
                "It is a compressed archive that contains every map and every guide page that the site has to offer",
                "It is a report written by the website itself that lists which of its pages have been visited lately"
              ],
              "answer": "It is a shopping list of the exact files to fetch, so that we can check the list before downloading",
              "explanation": "Discovery and downloading are separate steps. The list lets you see exactly what would be fetched, and it can be reused or edited."
            },
            {
              "question": "The atlas page lists five maps, but the harvester only keeps three. How does it decide?",
              "options": [
                "It opens each map and compares the picture with other maps of the game that it has already seen",
                "It asks an AI model to look at the thumbnails and judge which ones appear to belong to Ember Quest",
                "It keeps the three newest files on the page, based on the date that each one was last uploaded",
                "It keeps only the links whose file names start with the prefix we gave it, which is EmberQuest-"
              ],
              "answer": "It keeps only the links whose file names start with the prefix we gave it, which is EmberQuest-",
              "explanation": "A plain name rule does the work. It is predictable, and anyone can read the list afterwards and see exactly why each file is on it."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the purpose of the sha256 fingerprint recorded for each downloaded file?",
              "options": [
                "It encrypts each file so that nobody else is able to open it without first entering a password",
                "It lets us prove later exactly which version of a file our facts came from, as any change alters it",
                "It tells the website who downloaded the file, so that the owner of the site is able to contact us",
                "It shrinks each downloaded file so that the whole collection takes up less room on the hard drive"
              ],
              "answer": "It lets us prove later exactly which version of a file our facts came from, as any change alters it",
              "explanation": "A fingerprint is computed from a file's contents. If even one letter changes, so does the fingerprint, which makes it good evidence of what we actually collected."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-04-reading",
          "title": "Module 4: Stages 2 and 3, Reading and Organizing. From a Pile of Files to Facts",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "Watch the parser sort the pile, set aside a page about the wrong game, catch two sources disagreeing about a price, and report what is still missing. Then see the facts become a connected knowledge graph.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The problem with what people write</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Our folder is full of pages written for people, and people are inconsistent. They disagree. They write about other versions of a game. They misremember. If a program copied everything without thinking, the result would be confidently wrong.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">So the parser doesn't just read. It does four separate jobs:</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin:14px 0;align-items:stretch;\"><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 1</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Sort</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Work out what each file is, and set aside anything about the wrong game.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 2</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Read tables</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Copy numbers exactly, using ordinary code.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 3</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Compare</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Flag it when two sources disagree.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 4</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Report gaps</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">List what's still missing, in plain English.</div></div></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: the whole process</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Follow one single fact through this clip: <strong>the price of the Iron Sword</strong>.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/parse.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: the weapons page shows the Iron Sword at 40 gold. The parser sorts the files and skips a remake page that says 99. A player notes page says 45, and the parser reports the disagreement. The Iron Sword then appears as a structured fact with a price of 40.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">One price, three pages, three different answers, and how the parser handles each. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The main guide says the Iron Sword costs 40 gold.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The <code>scan</code> command sorts the files. The page about the Anniversary Edition remake is marked <code>skip</code>, because its prices belong to a different game. It says 99.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The <code>parse</code> command reads every table it can.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">A player's notes page says 45. The parser keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the disagreement for a human to look at.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The Iron Sword is now a structured fact: price 40, with a note of the page it came from.</li></ol></details></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 1: Sorting the pile</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The <code>scan</code> command looks at each file's type, picture size, name and page title, then gives its verdict. Here are a few of its lines:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>use   area-map    256x192   EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png\nuse   world-map   256x192   EmberQuest-World.png\nskip  document              guide/anniversary-prices.html\n      (Title names another edition or a mod, so its stats and prices are not used.)\nuse   walkthrough           guide/walkthrough.html</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">It spotted the remake because the page title mentions an \"Anniversary Edition\". Without this step, the shop in our rebuilt game would charge 99 gold for a sword that should cost 40.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 2: Reading tables with code, not AI</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">It's tempting to hand everything to an AI and ask it to read. We don't, for tables. AI models can quietly change a number, and they may \"remember\" facts from other versions of a game rather than reading what's on the page. Tables go through ordinary code, which finds the column called \"Price\" and copies exactly what it says.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">AI is useful for flowing paragraphs, like a walkthrough, where the facts aren't in neat rows. That's where our safety check comes in.</p><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #38bdf8;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#38bdf8;\">The quote check: no quote, no fact</strong><br>When the AI reads a paragraph, it must also hand back the <strong>exact sentence</strong> that supports each fact. Our code then searches the original file for that sentence, word for word. If it isn't there, the fact is thrown away. It's like asking a student to cite the page, then actually turning to that page to look. On the real Final Fantasy run, this check threw out 4 facts the AI could not back up.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 3: Catching disagreements</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">This line really did come out of the parser in the clip:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Iron Sword: price is 40 in guide/weapons.html but 45 in guide/shopkeeper-notes.html.</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Notice what it <em>doesn't</em> do. It doesn't quietly pick one, or average them to 42. It uses the page written for this version of the game, and leaves a clear note saying who disagreed. A person can then settle it. Real guides do this constantly: Final Fantasy's sources disagreed on 5 equipment numbers, such as whether one sword costs 5,000 or 6,000 gold.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 4: Knowing what you don't know</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The most valuable output might be the list of what's missing. The <code>coverage</code> report compares what we have to what a game needs. Here is a shortened version:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Ember Quest (Retro (1989)): 25 nodes\n  ok         1  World map reference\n  ok         2  Town, castle &amp; dungeon map references\n  MISSING    0  Places\n  MISSING    3  Weapons\n  ok         2  Spells\n  MISSING    0  Shops &amp; inns\n  MISSING    0  Story milestones\n\nStill to collect by hand:\n  - a walkthrough that names each place\n  - a weapon table with stats and prices\n  - a track list saying where each track plays</code></pre><div style=\"border-left:4px solid #f0b429;background:rgba(56,189,248,.07);padding:12px 16px;margin:14px 0;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;\"><strong style=\"color:#f0b429;\">Why does it say MISSING with 3 weapons?</strong><br>The bar is set for a full-size game, and Ember Quest is deliberately tiny. Several others are empty because the labs skip the AI step (<code>--no-agent</code>), so nothing reads the walkthrough's paragraphs. For a real game, this list becomes your to-do list. It's far better for a tool to say \"I don't have this\" than to guess.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Stage 3: Organizing into a knowledge graph</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Finally, the facts are saved as connected nodes. Here is the real node for the Iron Sword, with a few bookkeeping lines left out:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>{\n  \"@id\": \"urn:robos:jrpg:ember-quest:item:iron-sword\",\n  \"dcterms:title\": \"Iron Sword\",\n  \"robos:itemCategory\": \"weapon\",\n  \"jrpg:usableBy\": [\"Warden\"],\n  \"robos:attackBonus\": 8,\n  \"robos:cost\": 40,\n  \"jrpg:source\": { \"@id\": \"urn:robos:jrpg:ember-quest:source:guide-weapons-html\" }\n}</code></pre><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">This line</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>@id</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A unique name for this item, so nothing can be confused with it. Like a passport number.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>dcterms:title</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Its human-readable name.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>jrpg:usableBy</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A link to the class that can use it.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>robos:attackBonus</code>, <code>robos:cost</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Its damage and its price.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>jrpg:source</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A link to the source file it came from.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Ember Quest's graph has just 25 of these nodes. Final Fantasy's has 1,206. They work in exactly the same way.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Sort the pile with scan",
            "Parse it into facts",
            "Find the disagreement yourself",
            "Read the report of what is missing"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Sort the pile with scan",
              "description": "Ask the parser what it makes of each file. Look for the `skip` lines and read the reason beside them.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js scan ~/ember-quest/collection",
              "expected": "A line per file. `guide/anniversary-prices.html` is marked `skip`, with a note that its title names another edition. The two `.json` files are also skipped, because they are the harvester's own paperwork rather than guide material.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scan-sets-remake-aside.png",
              "imageAlt": "The scan output with the anniversary prices page marked skip, beside that page showing a price of 99",
              "caption": "The remake says 99 gold, and the parser won't use it."
            },
            {
              "title": "Parse it into facts",
              "description": "This reads every table and builds the knowledge graph. `--no-agent` means it uses plain code only, with no AI, so it needs no account and costs nothing.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph \\\n  --title \"Ember Quest\" --edition \"Retro (1989)\" --no-agent",
              "expected": "A progress line for each page, such as `guide/bestiary.html: charts have 3 enemies`, followed by the report starting `Ember Quest (Retro (1989)): 25 nodes`."
            },
            {
              "title": "Find the disagreement yourself",
              "description": "Search the parser's output for the conflicts section. On Windows, open `parsed.json` in any text editor and search for \"conflicts\" instead.",
              "code": "grep -A2 '\"conflicts\"' ~/ember-quest/kgraph/parsed.json",
              "expected": "You see the exact line from the lesson: `Iron Sword: price is 40 in guide/weapons.html but 45 in guide/shopkeeper-notes.html.`",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/parser-flags-conflict.png",
              "imageAlt": "A player notes page showing 45 gold, with the parser reporting that the price is 40 in one file and 45 in another",
              "caption": "The disagreement as seen in the recording."
            },
            {
              "title": "Read the report of what is missing",
              "description": "Print the report on its own, and read the \"Still to collect by hand\" section. Imagine you were really doing this: that's your shopping list for the next trip.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph",
              "expected": "A list of `ok` and `MISSING` lines, then \"Still to collect by hand\" and \"Notes\". The notes mention the skipped page, the price disagreement, and that the AI step was left out.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/iron-sword-as-data.png",
              "imageAlt": "The Iron Sword shown as a structured fact with its damage, price and source page",
              "caption": "The Iron Sword, now a fact that programs can use."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The Iron Sword is 40 gold on the guide, 45 in a player's notes and 99 on a remake page. What does the parser do?",
              "options": [
                "It takes an average of all three numbers, so that the final price is fair to all of the sources",
                "It uses 99 because that was the most recently written page and so is probably the most accurate",
                "It skips the remake, keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the 45 as a flagged disagreement",
                "It stops the entire process with an error, and refuses to continue until a person fixes the pages"
              ],
              "answer": "It skips the remake, keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the 45 as a flagged disagreement",
              "explanation": "Each kind of problem gets its own treatment. A different game is set aside, and a real disagreement is recorded for a person while the most trustworthy source wins."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why are number tables read with ordinary code rather than by an AI model?",
              "options": [
                "Ordinary code copies exactly what the column says, whereas an AI may change or \"remember\" a number",
                "AI models are not able to read any kind of table at all, because tables are stored in special files",
                "Ordinary code is much more creative, so it fills in any numbers that happen to be missing from a table",
                "AI models are only available to very large companies and cannot be used on a home computer at all"
              ],
              "answer": "Ordinary code copies exactly what the column says, whereas an AI may change or \"remember\" a number",
              "explanation": "For exact numbers, you want a tool that can only copy. An AI that has read about many versions of a game might give a plausible number from the wrong one."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does the quote check stop an AI model from inventing facts?",
              "options": [
                "It asks the AI model the same question three times, and only keeps those answers that all match",
                "It sends each of the facts to a person, who must read them all and approve them one after another",
                "It compares the facts with what other fans have said about the game on the wider public internet",
                "The AI must return the exact supporting sentence, and code checks that it is really in the source file"
              ],
              "answer": "The AI must return the exact supporting sentence, and code checks that it is really in the source file",
              "explanation": "Like checking a citation by turning to the page. If the sentence is not literally in the file, the fact is dropped."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-05-rebuilding",
          "title": "Module 5: Stage 4, Rebuilding. Turning Facts into a Playable Game",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "How a picture of a map becomes a grid of numbers a game can walk on, how a price in a table becomes a real shop, and where new artwork comes in. Then play the result yourself.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The big idea: a map is a grid of numbers</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Look at this picture of Ashford Town. To you, it's a village. To a game, a picture is useless: it can't tell a path from a pond. So we cut the picture into its 16-by-16-pixel tiles, give each different tile a number, and write out the whole town as a grid.</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px;align-items:center;justify-content:center;margin:14px 0;\"><div style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png\" alt=\"The map of Ashford Town: a town with a shop, a pond, a chest, a road and a cave, surrounded by trees\" style=\"width:384px;max-width:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The picture we collected</div></div><div style=\"text-align:center;\"><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:0 auto;font:12px monospace;\" aria-label=\"Ashford Town as a grid of tile numbers\"><tr><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;